Trace comparison
  The RIP/next-RIP sequences have a common prefix of 1,855,663 retirements. At RIP 0xffffffff8270ba55 (memcmp+0x65), run 1 takes the unequal-byte exit; run 2 branches to 0xffffffff8270ba40 for one more seven-instruction byte-compare iteration. The same +7 path occurs at trace indices 1,855,663 and 1,860,660 in run 1 (corresponding run 2 indices are shifted by 0 and 7), explaining the exact +14 record-count delta. After deleting these two insertions, all RIP/next-RIP transitions align. Aligned control-flow metadata does not: 100 records differ in captured flags/metadata and are not normalized or declared identical.
  Debug ELF disassembly resolves the path as __parse_crashkernel -> get_last_crashkernel.constprop.0 -> strstr("crashkernel=") -> memcmp. Both exact guest command lines are 155 bytes and do not contain "crashkernel="; strstr checks 144 candidate starts. Astra independently checked all 2x144 byte comparisons and branch flags with zero mismatches; candidate 85 was the only candidate with a different equal-prefix length. At zero-based candidate 85, run 1 has "5d3f" and run 2 has "ca02". Run 2's 'c' matches the first byte of the needle, then the next byte differs, adding one seven-instruction memcmp-loop pass in each of the two parser calls. This explains the local +14 retired instructions and does not explain the remaining machine/UART differences.

The original source comparator remains false with exactly two unexplained fields: run[1].machine_observations and run[1].ordered_uart_bytes after nonce normalization. The two manifests are diagnostic_not_counted because both `linux_norandmaps` and `linux_no_randomize_kstack_offset` are true; Stage4A itself is the counted CPUID profile, and `diagnostic_past_c7` is false. Their observed syscall order is [83,165,257,0,3,35,1,39,60], while the current S4-39j eligibility gate expects [83,165,257,0,3,1,39,35,60]. This is a separate eligibility failure; correcting syscall order alone does not clear the explicit diagnostic flags.

