Next valid candidate: use the verified canonical S4-39j input bytes and Stage4A counted profile with `--set-cpl0 --user-mode`, RAM128, and the 3B budget; omit `--linux-norandmaps`, `--linux-no-randomize-kstack-offset`, and `--diagnostic-past-c7`. Run a new common-ID N=2 series and let series mode generate fresh nonces and set acceptance. A successful strict `matches=true` / empty-unexplained result is still required; the old A/B comparison does not close S5-07. No comparator or eligibility gate was changed.

Later diagnostic frontier in the original source pair, if needed after the counted series: callback 9,612 is at quantum 49,562 and RIP 0xffffffff8184aeb3 in both runs; callback 9,613 completes quantum 49,563. For that next quantum, the pre-dispatch charged-retirement gates are 1,214,375,282 (run 1) and 1,214,375,296 (run 2), with global callbacks 9,612→9,613. The quantum reports 5,277 instructions / through-count 1,214,380,559 / ending RDTSC RIP 0xffffffff8184f2f1 in run 1, and 23,463 / 1,214,398,759 / 0xffffffff8184aeb3 in run 2. Debug-ELF symbol/DWARF lookup places these sampled sites in `mm/slub.c` (`__refill_objects_any` and `get_from_any_partial`); this identifies an allocator-region sampling divergence, not the first differing instruction or a proven allocation decision. The 44 KiB memory-availability difference and exact branch cause remain unproven. Current schema-v13 hardcodes quantum 45,215 and cannot select this boundary; any future trace must preserve that schema-v13 meaning and add a separately gated target with per-run charged-retirement expectations. A 1.215-billion diagnostic budget may cover these existing through-counts but cannot replace counted 3B acceptance.

